<Project Sdk="Microsoft.NET.Sdk">

  <!--
    SMS.Application — the use cases. Both hosts run these, in-process.

      May reference:      Domain.
      Must not reference: Infrastructure, Web, Api.

    The Infrastructure rule is the one that matters, and it is the rule people argue with,
    so the reason is written where the temptation lives.

    A handler needs the database. The quick way to get it is a reference to Infrastructure
    and an injected DbContext. Do not. This layer declares what it needs as interfaces —
    IMasterStore, ISchoolStore, IUnitOfWork, ISchemaVersionSource — and Infrastructure
    implements them. Each host wires the two together at startup.

    What that buys: the use cases can be tested without a database, and a change of
    persistence is a change in one project. What it costs: a small interface per concern.
    These are named operations, not generic repositories — architecture section 10.

    Microsoft.Extensions.Identity.Core is allowed. UserManager<T> is a framework service,
    not infrastructure, and wrapping it would add a layer of ceremony around something the
    web host and the API both already depend on.

    Deployment mode (SingleTenant / MultiTenant) must never be read here. Grepping this
    project and Domain for it should return nothing — architecture section 8.
  -->

  <ItemGroup>
    <ProjectReference Include="..\SMS.Domain\SMS.Domain.csproj" />
  </ItemGroup>

  <ItemGroup>
    <PackageReference Include="Microsoft.Extensions.DependencyInjection.Abstractions" />
    <PackageReference Include="Microsoft.Extensions.Identity.Core" />
  </ItemGroup>

</Project>
